JOIN is used to combine rows from two or more tables based on a related column.
The most common join condition uses ON to specify how the tables are related.
INNER JOIN returns only the rows with matching values in both tables.
LEFT JOIN returns all rows from the left table and matching rows from the right table. If there is no match, the right table columns contain NULL.
RIGHT JOIN returns all rows from the right table and matching rows from the left table. If there is no match, the left table columns contain NULL.
FULL OUTER JOIN returns all rows from both tables. Unmatched rows from either table contain NULL for the missing side.
CROSS JOIN returns the Cartesian product, combining every row of the first table with every row of the second table.
SELF JOIN joins a table with itself using aliases.
The ON clause specifies the join condition, while USING can be used when both tables have the same column name.
Choosing the correct join type depends on whether you need only matching rows, all rows from one table, or all rows from both tables.
